    description: "FOUND 2026-09-01 by the module-key concept audit (cadence). Full write-up:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-module-key_09012026.md\n\nTHE GAP. receiver_type_hint is a REGISTERED edge-meta key (axis_meta_keys.py:1187)\ncarrying exactly the inferred receiver TYPE. It is stamped by SIX analyzers\n(java, kotlin, scala, swift, py, d_lang) and consumed by TWO linkers\n(linkers/inherited_calls.py, linkers/receiver_type_dispatch.py). It is NOT read\nby io_boundary.py, and NOT read by taint.py.\n\nThose two are precisely the consumers that need it. Both match a catalogue row\nwhose module, for a kind=method row, IS a receiver type -- and both decide\npackage-vs-type by ORTHOGRAPHY, because nothing tells them which the module slot\nholds. _module_matches' own docstring: \"The separator is therefore not reliable\nevidence of a package boundary. What *is* reliable is Go's naming convention:\npackage names are lowercase, exported type names are capitalised.\"\n\nSo the field that answers the question is stamped onto the edge and thrown away\nby the only two consumers whose correctness turns on it. This is WI-famig's\ndefect in the consumer direction: not a producer with no live consumer, but a\nconsumer ignoring a live producer.\n\nSTEP 4.5 FIVE-SHAPE TRACE, run against io_boundary.py and taint.py, since the\nclaim asserts non-consumption:\n  1. literal kwarg / literal key                -> zero hits\n  2. helper-call indirection (grep receiver_type) -> zero hits\n  3. assignment-form / variable key             -> zero hits; every meta.get in\n                                                   both files uses a literal key\n  4. f-string key                               -> zero hits\n  5. dict-subscript / iteration over meta       -> zero hits; the meta[...] and\n                                                   meta['...'] matches in both\n                                                   files are COMMENTS about other\n                                                   keys (bridge_kind, protocol)\nThe key is NOT dead vocabulary -- it has live consumers elsewhere. It is unread\nAT THIS SEAM.\n\nMEASURED ON THE SHAPE THAT MATTERS. Purpose-built java probe, shipped analyzer:\n\n    name=nextLine  module_path=java.util.Scanner      receiver_type_hint='Scanner'\n    name=readLine  module_path=java.io.BufferedReader receiver_type_hint='BufferedReader'\n\nREFUTES A TEMPTING OVER-CLAIM, recorded so nobody re-derives it: java's module\nkey is NOT what blocks WI-lipis's java arm. Java resolves sc.nextLine() to\njava.util.Scanner correctly, and the module slot and receiver_type_hint agree.\nWI-lipis's java blocker is the argument hop (System.in is an argument to the\nCONSTRUCTOR, not to the rowed method), exactly as already filed.\n\nWHERE THIS WOULD ACTUALLY PAY. The languages that stamp receiver_type_hint are\njava/kotlin/scala/swift/py. Swift is the one where the module slot is measured\nWRONG at scale (INV-kotob: 1,102 refs / 26% of objc+swift external refs carry a\nreceiver VARIABLE name), and swift stamps receiver_type_hint. So swift is the\ncase where a correct type is available on the edge while the module slot holds a\nlocal identifier, and the matcher reads the identifier. That is the highest-value\nfirst target, not java.\n\nNOT A FIX PROPOSAL YET, deliberately. Wiring a second hint source into\nlookup_with_module is a precision change in the ADDING direction and must be\nmeasured as one: an A/B on a cold cohort with the added/removed split reported\nseparately, per the WI-lipis protocol. Note the direction differs from WI-lipis:\nhere a correct type can only make the module filter STRICTER (refusing matches it\ncurrently accepts), so `added=0` is expected and `removed` is the number to\nadjudicate row by row.\n\nGO IS OUT OF SCOPE and this matters for INV-dijor: go does NOT stamp\nreceiver_type_hint, so this would not fix the sops context.String false positive.\nTwo different remedies, filed separately."  # cf28
